## Why This Question Is Asked
This question assesses practical memory management skills and understanding of lock-free data structures—essential competencies for systems programming and performance-critical applications.

## Key Concepts
- **Lock-free free-list design**: Using an atomic head pointer enables concurrent allocation/deallocation without locks
- **Memory layout**: Fixed-size 64-byte blocks with the next pointer as the first field ensure proper alignment on 64-bit systems
- **ABA problem**: Tagged pointers (using the unused bits in a 64-bit pointer) can prevent ABA issues in high-contention scenarios
- **Contention testing**: Multi-threaded stress tests verify correctness under concurrent access patterns

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct Block {
    struct Block* next;
    unsigned char payload[56];
} Block;

static _Atomic(Block*) free_head;

Block* allocate(void) {
    Block* head;
    Block* new_head;
    do {
        head = atomic_load_explicit(&free_head, memory_order_acquire);
        if (!head) return NULL;
        new_head = head->next;
    } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak_explicit(
        &free_head, &head, new_head,
        memory_order_release, memory_order_relaxed));
    return head;
}

void free_block(void* ptr) {
    Block* block = (Block*)ptr;
    Block* head;
    do {
        head = atomic_load_explicit(&free_head, memory_order_acquire);
        block->next = head;
    } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak_explicit(
        &free_head, &head, block,
        memory_order_release, memory_order_relaxed));
}
```

## Test Plan (4 Threads)
1. **Initialization**: Preallocate all N blocks, verify free_head points to the full list
2. **Basic correctness**: Single-threaded allocate/free sequences, verify all blocks returned
3. **Concurrent stress**: 4 threads repeatedly allocate and free blocks; track allocation counts to ensure no leaks or double-frees
4. **Boundary conditions**: Allocate until empty, verify NULL returned; free all blocks, verify full recovery
5. **Data integrity**: Write patterns to payload, free, allocate fresh blocks, verify previous data is not present

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of NUMA-aware memory management and lock-free structures beyond simple queues. Candidates must justify locality decisions, reclamation delay trade-offs, and fragmentation management under mixed workloads. The prompt also probes API design, minimal synchronization strategies, and practical benchmarking methodology.

## Key Concepts

- **NUMA locality**: Allocations should prefer the local node to avoid expensive remote memory access
- **Slab allocator design**: Pre-allocates memory in fixed-size slabs per object size class
- **Size bucketing**: Groups objects into size classes (32–128B, 1–4KB) to reduce fragmentation
- **Lock-free free lists**: CAS-based singly-linked lists enable concurrent access without heavy locking
- **Cross-node reclamation**: Deferred reclamation via epoch orquEUe avoids false sharing and reduces ping-ponging
- **Alignment and false sharing**: Cache-line padding prevents unrelated objects from sharing the same line
- **TLB/cache effects**: Slab locality improves cache hit rates; large pages (huge TLB entries) reduce TLB misses for bulk operations

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

typedef enum { SMALL_32B, SMALL_64B, SMALL_128B, MEDIUM_1KB, MEDIUM_4KB, NUM_BUCKETS } bucket_t;

typedef struct free_node {
    struct free_node *next;
} free_node_t;

typedef struct {
    free_node_t *head;
    _Atomic uint64_t epoch;
    char pad[64 - sizeof(void*) - sizeof(_Atomic uint64_t)];
} alignas(64) lockfree_freelist_t;

typedef struct {
    lockfree_freelist_t freelists[NUM_BUCKETS];
    void *slab_start;
    size_t slab_size;
    int node_id;
    bool preallocate;
} alignas(64) numa_slab_cache_t;

typedef struct {
    numa_slab_cache_t *caches;
    int num_nodes;
    size_t page_size;
    void *(*numa_alloc)(size_t size, int node);
    void (*numa_free)(void *ptr, size_t size);
} numa_allocator_t;

numa_allocator_t *numa_allocator_create(int num_nodes, void *(*alloc_fn)(size_t, int), void (*free_fn)(void*, size_t));
void *numa_alloc(numa_allocator_t *alloc, size_t size, int node_hint);
void numa_free(numa_allocator_t *alloc, void *ptr, size_t size, int target_node);
void numa_allocator_destroy(numa_allocator_t *alloc);
```

## Fragmentation Considerations

- **Internal fragmentation**: Inherent in fixed-size bucketing; acceptable given latency requirements
- **External fragmentation**: Mitigated by pre-allocated slabs and lazy reclamation (freed objects held until next allocation)
- **Cross-node fragmentation**: Remote frees accumulate in target node queues; monitored via periodic compaction

## TLB/Cache Effects

- Slab allocation ensures objects within a slab share TLB entries
- Cache-line-aligned objects prevent false sharing in multiprocess workloads
- Large pages (2MB/1GB) recommended for bulk allocations to reduce TLB pressure
- NUMA interleaving disabled for hot paths; manual placement preferred

## Microbenchmark Plan

| Test | Workload | Metrics |
|------|----------|---------|
| 1. Local-only | Allocate/free on local node | Latency p50/p99/p999 |
| 2. Remote-heavy | 90% cross-node frees | Remote access ratio, latency delta |
| 3. Mixed sizes | Uniform distribution across buckets | Fragmentation index |
| 4. Contention | High thread count, shared buckets | Throughput, lock convoy detection |
| 5. Reclaim storm | Bulk free across nodes | Reclamation latency, stall time |

Measure under steady-state after warmup; use perf stat for cache-miss rates and TLB miss ratios.

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design crash-consistent data structures for persistent memory, requiring reasoning about durability guarantees, lock-free synchronization patterns, and recovery mechanisms after power loss.

## Key Concepts

- **Crash-consistency in PMEM**: Using CLWB (Cache Line Write Back) to flush modified cache lines to persistent media without invalidating, combined with SFENCE to enforce memory ordering and guarantee durability
- **Lock-free MPSC patterns**: Utilizing atomic operations on persistent indices to enable multiple producers to enqueue concurrently without locks
- **Recovery mechanisms**: Detecting and repairing partially written slots, reconstructing in-flight state after system restart

